{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And I turned and came down from the mountain.</b> On that very day, on the third day of the week, the tenth of Tishrei. As it is said in the pericah of תשא: ״ויהי ברדת משה מן ההר״ etc. Even though he was delayed somewhat on the tenth of Tishrei when he came down from the mountain and brought the tablets, this is not counted among the third set of forty days, for they had already been completed at daybreak of the tenth of Tishrei. It follows that the beginning of his withdrawal was on the second day of the week, which is Rosh Chodesh Sivan of the giving of the Torah, on which Moses began to fast in order to purge his eating, to be like the ministering angels, to receive the Torah in purity; and the last ascent was on the fifth day of the week, on the twenty-ninth of Av; and the end of his fasts was on the second day of the week, the tenth of Tishrei. Therefore the public adopted the practice of fasting on Monday and Thursday and Monday, following this sequence from Rosh Chodesh Sivan until the tenth of Tishrei. Thus I found."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:21.577610663Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}